---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Header from '../../../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../../../components/Footer.astro';
import PageHero from '../../../components/PageHero.astro';
import CTABand from '../../../components/CTABand.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Root Canal Aftercare | Center for Smile Enhancement"
  description="Aftercare instructions after root canal therapy at the Center for Smile Enhancement in Minnetonka, MN, including pain management tips and when to call us."
>
  <Header />

  <main id="main-content">
    <PageHero
      eyebrow="Aftercare Instructions"
      title="Root Canal Aftercare"
      lede="Root canal therapy is completed in two appointments. Here's what to expect and how to care for your tooth in between."
      breadcrumbs={[
        { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
        { label: 'Aftercare' },
        { label: 'Root Canals' },
      ]}
    />

    <section class="section-white">
      <div class="site-container section-py">
        <div class="article-prose">
          <h2>What to Expect</h2>
          <p>
            Root canal therapy is typically completed in two stages. At the first appointment, we remove the infected tissue from inside the tooth and place a medicated dressing before sealing the tooth with a temporary filling. At the second appointment, we remove the temporary filling and complete the permanent restoration (please note there are additional fees for the permanent restoration).
          </p>
          <p>
            You may notice an unusual taste from the medication placed inside the tooth — this is normal, but let us know about it at your next visit. You'll likely remain numb for a few hours after your procedure, the injection site may feel tender or bruised for several days, and some deep discomfort in the jaw for a few days is also common.
          </p>

          <h2>Do's and Don'ts</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>Avoid chewing on the treated tooth until your second appointment is complete.</li>
            <li>The temporary filling may wear away slightly at the surface — that's normal. As long as the opening stays sealed, your tooth is protected.</li>
            <li>Call us right away if the treated tooth feels high, or touches the opposing tooth when you bite down.</li>
            <li>If we've prescribed an antibiotic, complete the full course as directed, even if you're feeling better.</li>
          </ul>

          <h2>Pain Management</h2>
          <p>
            Over-the-counter options such as ibuprofen (Advil), naproxen sodium (Aleve), or acetaminophen (Tylenol) work best when taken before discomfort sets in. If you need more than a single medication provides, an Advil-and-Tylenol combination — alternating between the two on a regular schedule — is an effective approach many patients use for post-procedure discomfort. Ask our team for guidance on timing a combination schedule that works for you.
          </p>

          <h2>When to Call Us</h2>
          <p>
            Call our office at <a href="tel:9525939310">952-593-9310</a> any time you have questions or concerns — including if your bite feels off, discomfort is more than mild, or the sealed opening in your tooth is exposed.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <CTABand
      heading="Questions About Your Recovery?"
      sub="If anything feels off after your root canal, please reach out — we're here to help."
      primaryCta={{ label: 'Contact Our Office', href: '/contact/' }}
      secondaryCta={{ label: 'Call 952-593-9310', href: 'tel:9525939310' }}
    />
  </main>

  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>
